    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_WindowsConsoleReader.readinto` function is designed to read a specified number of bytes from a Windows console into a buffer provided by the caller. It exists to handle the intricacies of reading from a Windows console, particularly when dealing with UTF-16-LE encoded text. The function takes a single parameter, `b`, which is a buffer object that must be writable. This buffer is where the read bytes will be stored. The function returns the number of bytes actually read into the buffer. It relies on the `get_buffer` helper function to obtain a writable buffer view and the `ReadConsoleW` function from the Windows API to perform the actual read operation. If an odd number of bytes is requested, the function raises a `ValueError` because UTF-16-LE encoding requires reading an even number of bytes. If the read operation is interrupted, the function waits briefly for a `KeyboardInterrupt`. If the read operation fails, it raises an `OSError` with the Windows error code. If the first byte in the buffer is an EOF marker, the function returns 0, indicating no data was read. The function has no side effects other than modifying the provided buffer and potentially raising exceptions.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\